00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:17.720
Now, speaking of vapor intrusion work, consider a former dry cleaning site in Southern Ontario

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:24.820
that you visit in early June. Sub-slab vapor results from March sampling appear markedly lower

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:31.060
than the follow-up round completed last week. No interim remedial work occurred between the two

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:37.540
rounds. The difference arises because frozen ground and reduced barometric pressure fluctuations in

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:43.860
winter suppress advective flow through the slab. Spring thaw and increasing wind-driven pressure

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:53.540
differentials enhance vapor entry once conditions change. CCME soil vapor guidance and Ontario OREG 153-04

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:58.820
both require practitioners to account for seasonal pressure differentials when interpreting

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:05.600
results against tier one or site specific standards. Experienced assessors examine barometric pressure

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:12.700
logs and slab integrity data alongside concentration trends. They avoid treating any single round as

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:19.180
representative on its own. The most common mistake is to average winter and spring data sets without

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:25.320
documenting the physical drivers of variability. That approach can lead to underestimation of risk

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:31.620
at the building envelope. The recommended fix is to require a minimum 48-hour pressure differential

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:37.840
record with each vapor sampling event. Any round collected when ground is frozen or snow covered

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:44.180
should be flagged for potential resampling under unfrozen conditions. If you are working on vapor

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:49.860
intrusion assessments in Ontario, you will want to review current project files for any assessments

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:54.620
completed between December and March that may require seasonal confirmation sampling.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:00.320
Update internal field forms to capture barometric pressure and ground condition metadata

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:07.160
on all soil vapor events. Brief project managers on the need to schedule at least one unfrozen

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:13.120
condition vapor round for sites where winter data form the sole basis of risk conclusions.

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:19.980
Cross-check laboratory chain of custody records to confirm holding times were met for any delayed

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:27.740
spring sample shipments. Monitor Canada Gazette Part 1 for any SEPA 2024 amendment notices

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:35.320
expected in the next 14 days. Track provincial ministry postings for routine CSR annual reporting

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:41.700
reminders applicable to schedule two sites. Prepare equipment calibration schedules ahead

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:48.360
of peak summer ground water and soil vapor programs. Tomorrow watch for any SEPA amendment notices

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:54.480
that may appear in the next Canada Gazette release. That's environmental intelligence for today.

